    body: "Internet bills creep up. The promo rate you signed up for runs out after a year or two, and the price quietly climbs. A bill that started at $49.99 a month often grows to $89.99 or more without you ever agreeing to a raise. The good news is internet companies expect customers to call and ask for a better rate. Most of them have a department called Customer Retention or Loyalty whose entire job is to keep you from canceling. If you call the right way, you can often save $20 to $40 a month, which adds up to $240 to $480 a year. You do not need to be a smooth talker. You do not need to threaten to cancel. You only need a calm voice, a few key facts, and a short script. This guide gives you the exact words to say, the right person to ask for, and a fallback plan if the first agent says no. We cover the four big providers most older adults use (Xfinity/Comcast, Spectrum, AT&T, and Verizon Fios) and the senior discount programs each one offers. We also cover the federal Lifeline program, which can knock $9.25 off your monthly bill if you qualify based on income. Before you call, gather a recent bill, jot down what your neighbors pay, and pick a time when you are not rushed. The call usually takes 20 to 40 minutes. Have a glass of water nearby. By the end of this guide, you will know what to ask for, what to listen for, and when to say yes or hang up and try again. Saving money on your internet does not require tech skills. It requires a quiet hour and a willingness to say, \"Can you do better?\"",
