    body: "Zelle is a fast money-transfer service built into most major banking apps, including Chase, Bank of America, and Wells Fargo. It moves money between bank accounts in minutes. That speed is exactly why scammers favor it. A Zelle payment behaves like handing someone cash: once it is sent, the money is usually gone. Your bank has limited ability to get it back, even if you report it right away.\n\nThe Consumer Financial Protection Bureau and FTC have both warned about Zelle scams repeatedly. The pattern is always the same. Someone contacts you claiming to be your bank's fraud department, a family member in trouble, an online seller, or a romantic interest. Then they create urgency, pushing you to send money through Zelle right now.\n\nOne rule prevents most of these scams. Zelle is for paying people you already know and trust — splitting a dinner bill with a friend, or paying rent to a landlord you have met. It is not for paying strangers, online sellers you have never dealt with, or anyone who contacts you first asking for money.\n\nIf you use Zelle with family, keep using it. It is a legitimate and convenient tool. This guide is here to help you pause and check in the specific situations scammers rely on.",
